sec-signin-eyebrow = Signing in and registering
sec-signin-title = Central verifies your password without receiving it
sec-signin-lead = Baylee signs you in with OPAQUE (RFC 9807), a zero-knowledge login proof. Your device and Central exchange three messages, and your password is in none of them—not even as a plain hash.
sec-signin-c1 = Blinded request
sec-signin-t1 = Your device blinds the password.
sec-signin-s1 = It mixes the password with a fresh random value and sends only that blinded request. Your password cannot be read from it.
sec-signin-c2 = Central's answer
sec-signin-t2 = Central answers with a key of its own.
sec-signin-s2 = Central computes on the blinded request using a key only it holds and adds its half of a key exchange, without ever learning your password.
sec-signin-c3 = Argon2id · 64 MiB
sec-signin-t3 = Your device stretches the key locally.
sec-signin-s3 = It unblinds the answer and runs Argon2id on your device (64 MiB of memory, three passes). A mistyped password is caught right here before sign-in completes.
sec-signin-c4 = Proof
sec-signin-t4 = Your device sends a compact login proof.
sec-signin-s4 = With the right password your device reaches the same session key as Central and sends only a short 64-byte proof.
sec-signin-c5 = Session + wrapper
sec-signin-t5 = Central opens your session.
sec-signin-s5 = Back come your session (in the browser, a cookie scripts cannot read) and your master key in an encrypted wrapper that only opens on your device with the right password.
sec-signin-register = <b>When you register,</b> your device creates a random 256-bit master key, wraps one copy under your password and a second copy under your recovery code, and uploads only those sealed wrappers. Central never holds the key itself.
sec-memory-eyebrow = In memory
sec-memory-title = How your password and keys stay tidy in memory
sec-memory-lead = Your password is needed only once: to open your master key. Afterwards, Baylee clears working memory promptly so sensitive values do not linger.
sec-memory-c1 = Password
sec-memory-t1 = The page hands your password to a Worker.
sec-memory-s1 = The sign-in form passes it to an isolated background thread (a Worker), clears the input field, and drops its copy right away.
sec-memory-c2 = OPAQUE + Argon2id
sec-memory-t2 = The Worker computes, then zeroes out.
sec-memory-s2 = Its Rust WebAssembly code holds the password only until the OPAQUE step finishes, then overwrites the password, derived keys, and Argon2id's 64 MiB workspace.
sec-memory-c3 = Master key: opened, wiped
sec-memory-t3 = Your master key is opened, used and wiped.
sec-memory-s3 = The Worker opens it briefly to verify sign-in and derive your chat keys, then overwrites the master key immediately.
sec-memory-c4 = Chat keys, sealed
sec-memory-t4 = Only sealed chat keys stay for reloads.
sec-memory-s4 = So chats open smoothly after a reload without retyping your password, the Worker stores the chat keys in browser storage (IndexedDB) sealed with AES-256-GCM under a non-extractable key. Signing out deletes the entry right away.
sec-memory-c5 = Worker ended
sec-memory-t5 = The sign-in Worker shuts down.
sec-memory-s5 = Once sign-in is complete, the sign-in page ends its Worker and the browser frees that Worker's memory.
sec-memory-client = <b>In the Baylee desktop client,</b> your typed password stays in zeroized memory and is wiped right after sign-in without ever being saved. Your master key remains in client memory while signed in so the client can open your encrypted data.
sec-chat-eyebrow = Chat
sec-chat-title = Messages are sealed on your device
sec-chat-lead = Every message gets a fresh key of its own. Central stores and delivers sealed envelopes without being able to read them.
sec-chat-c1 = Text + one-time key
sec-chat-t1 = A fresh key for every message.
sec-chat-s1 = Your device generates a random one-time message key and encrypts the message text with XChaCha20-Poly1305.
sec-chat-c2 = Sealed wraps + signature
sec-chat-t2 = Sealed for each recipient and signed by you.
sec-chat-s2 = It wraps the message key individually for every chat key on the recipient's account (X25519) and signs the header and ciphertext with your chat key (Ed25519).
sec-chat-c3 = Ciphertext + wraps
sec-chat-t3 = Central stores the sealed envelope.
sec-chat-s3 = Central checks your signature and recipient list, then stores the ciphertext alongside the wraps. It has neither message keys nor your account keys.
sec-chat-c4 = Ciphertext + one wrap
sec-chat-t4 = Each contact receives only their own wrap.
sec-chat-s4 = When your contact fetches messages, Central delivers the encrypted message together with only the wrap sealed for that chat key.
sec-chat-c5 = Checked, opened
sec-chat-t5 = Check, unwrap, and read.
sec-chat-s5 = Your contact's device checks your signature, unwraps the message key with its own chat key, and decrypts the message.
sec-chat-meta = <b>What Central handles for delivery:</b> contacts, groups with names and members, display names, public chat keys, and delivery metadata such as sender, conversation, timestamp and size.
sec-chat-verify = <b>Easy key verification:</b> whenever you want extra assurance, you can compare a short safety number with your contact over another channel. If a contact's keys change later, Baylee pauses until you confirm them.
sec-pair-eyebrow = Signing in by link
sec-pair-title = Connect the Baylee client without retyping your password
sec-pair-lead = When you are signed in on the website, pairing passes your master key straight to the desktop client in an end-to-end sealed box, confirmed with a short verification code on both screens.
sec-pair-status = <b>Rollout note:</b> Central and Baylee's client library already complete this pairing flow together in automated tests. The website switch will go live alongside the upcoming client release.
sec-pair-c1 = Start pairing
sec-pair-t1 = The page opens a short-lived pairing.
sec-pair-s1 = Central issues a handle valid for five minutes, while the page creates a fresh one-time secret that never travels through Central.
sec-pair-c2 = Link with secret
sec-pair-t2 = A local link opens the client.
sec-pair-s2 = The link <code>baylee://pair</code> hands both the handle and the one-time secret from the page directly to the client on your device.
sec-pair-c3 = Key + short code
sec-pair-t3 = Both screens confirm with a short code.
sec-pair-s3 = The client replies with a fresh key pair and an HMAC-SHA-256 check using the secret. Both screens display the same short verification code so you can confirm at a glance.
sec-pair-c4 = Master key, sealed
sec-pair-t4 = The page seals the master key with HPKE.
sec-pair-s4 = With HPKE (RFC 9180), the page seals the master key both to the client's public key and the secret. Central holds the sealed box once and cannot open or forge it.
sec-pair-c5 = Session + seal
sec-pair-t5 = The client redeems it once.
sec-pair-s5 = It receives a session along with the seal, opens it with its private key and the secret, and cleans up the pairing record right away.
sec-pair-note = <b>Your password never enters this exchange:</b> the browser holds the master key only right after sign-in, and the paired session keeps the same age as that original browser sign-in.
sec-good-eyebrow = Good to know
sec-good-title = Good to know & Open Source
sec-good-recovery = <b>Keep a backup of your recovery code.</b> Because Central stores only sealed wrappers and never holds your keys, your password and your recovery code are the keys back into your encrypted data on a new device. Store a copy of your recovery code somewhere calm and trusted, such as a password manager or printed note.
sec-good-basics = <b>Published protocols and everyday device care.</b> Sign-in follows OPAQUE (RFC 9807) with Argon2id (RFC 9106), pairing seals with HPKE (RFC 9180), and chat uses XChaCha20-Poly1305, X25519 and Ed25519. Keeping your browser and operating system up to date protects what appears on screen before encryption begins.
sec-good-opensource = <b>Open source and transparent.</b> Central, the Baylee client and this website are all open source so anyone can inspect how every flow works. Spot something we can improve or have a question? <contact>Please tell us</contact>.
sec-docs-title = To read further
sec-docs = Full protocol details live in our project documents: <protocol>crypto protocol</protocol>, <chat>chat</chat>, <accounts>accounts and pairing</accounts> and <threats>threat model</threats>. For legal terms, read the <privacy>privacy notice</privacy>.
